## MCP OBO on Lakehouse: bypass path carries the forwarded user token

Context:
Explorer and Graph Chat already run Lakehouse / Unity Catalog reads
on-behalf-of the signed-in user, but MCP did not. MCP hits the public
`/api/v1/*` + `/graphql/*` surface, which `PermissionMiddleware` lists in
`_PERM_BYPASS_PREFIXES` (they skip the session role gate so programmatic /
MCP callers are not 302-redirected to `/access-denied`). The bypass branch
stamped an *empty* request identity, discarding the forwarded
`x-forwarded-access-token`, so `create_databricks_client` fell back to the
app service principal — MCP graph reads inherited the SP's UC rights instead
of the user's. This wires OBO on that path so MCP leverages OBO on Lakehouse
exactly like Explorer and Chat.

Changes:

1. `src/shared/fastapi/main.py`
   In the `_PERM_BYPASS_PREFIXES` branch of `PermissionMiddleware.dispatch`,
   read `x-forwarded-access-token` and bind it (with the forwarded email) into
   the request-scoped `RequestIdentity` / `request.state.user_token`. The
   session role gate stays bypassed (roles remain `""`, no redirect), but the
   forwarded user token is now available to `get_graphdb` →
   `create_databricks_client`, so Lakehouse reads on `/api/v1/digitaltwin/*`
   and `/graphql/*` run OBO. No token → SP fallback (unchanged).
2. `tests/units/auth/test_permission_middleware.py`
   Added `TestBypassBindsForwardedTokenForOBO`: the forwarded token is bound
   into the request identity on `/api/*` and `/graphql/*` while roles stay
   empty; a request with no token keeps an empty token (SP fallback).

